infographic compare-binary-horizontal-underline-text-vs
data
  title Serializing the response: generic vs schema
  desc Same result object turned into bytes — the CPU the request always pays after the handler returns
  compares
    - label Generic JSON.stringify
      children
        - label Synchronous, O(payload size)
          desc Cost scales with object size and is invisible until the result set grows
        - label Blocks the event loop
          desc A 5,000-row payload costs several ms of pure CPU, stalling every other request
        - label Baseline speed (1×), any shape
          desc No schema needed — the default, and the hidden cost in the 40%-slower endpoint
    - label Schema serializer
      children
        - label Precompiles the response shape
          desc fast-json-stringify, Protobuf, MessagePack know the fields ahead of time
        - label 2–4× faster
          desc Why high-throughput APIs declare response schemas on hot endpoints
        - label Needs a declared schema
          desc Up-front shape declaration that pays off on hot, high-volume endpoints
theme
  palette #f43f5e #14b8a6
